**Omitted:** `execution`. Its records are a bare handoff plus old closed missions — no sessions, files, assets, or deliverables in the window, and no first/last activity timestamps. There's nothing there to read honestly, so it gets no synthesis and falls back to the renderer's facts-only default.

**What the bundle actually says**, in one line: nearly every live thread is *built and unsent*. Approved reels waiting to go to Jen, a Claim Check funnel staged but unpublished, brand teardowns finished as lead magnets and never sent, an offer OS fully specified that has never met a buyer, a trailer prompt never rendered. The two threads with genuine quality signal (angle-map brief, trailer package) both self-scored under bar. That's the through-line I put in the board lede.

**Constraint compliance:** verified programmatically — no digits, no path/filename fragments in any value; key set is a strict subset of the five contract slots; all slugs valid.

`why` is written only where the renderer will actually build a decision item — `extract-forge` and `01-listing-content` (unfinished lines) and `04-briefs`, `teardown-1-fresh`, `trailer-treatment` (idle-past-threshold). No thread had an open mission or a blocked handoff, so index `"0"` is the only live index anywhere.

★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────
`sec_decision` builds its items in a fixed order and only then overlays `why` by stringified index — so the index isn't a stable identity, it's a position in a list whose length depends on thread state. Writing `"1"` for a thread that yields one item silently drops the text; writing `"0"` for a thread whose item order shifts next sweep silently re-attaches your reasoning to a different action. That's why the whys here are worded to justify the *class* of action (park-vs-resume, promote-vs-close) rather than the specific sentence the renderer will pair them with.
─────────────────────────────────────────────────

I didn't run `mission_brief.py build` — you asked for the JSON only.
